   $q$Read the sentences: "The orchard planted twelve heirloom apple varieties. Three of them had nearly disappeared from local farms." Which choice most effectively combines the sentences?$q$, $q$[{"text":"The orchard planted twelve heirloom apple varieties, three of them had nearly disappeared from local farms.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"comma-splice-created"},{"text":"The orchard planted twelve heirloom apple varieties; three of which had nearly disappeared from local farms.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"semicolon-before-dependent-clause"},{"text":"The orchard planted twelve heirloom apple varieties, three of which had nearly disappeared from local farms.","is_correct":true},{"text":"The orchard planted twelve heirloom apple varieties three of them had nearly disappeared from local farms.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"fused-sentence"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$The orchard planted twelve heirloom apple varieties, three of which had nearly disappeared from local farms.$q$, $q$"Three of which" makes the second idea a dependent clause, so a comma correctly attaches it to the main clause. "Three of them" is an independent clause, so joining it with only a comma creates a comma splice.$q$, 'draft', null)
