   $q$Which statement explains why $\frac{2}{3} = \frac{8}{12}$?$q$, $q$[{"text":"Both fractions have even numerators.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"used-surface-feature"},{"text":"$\\frac{8}{12}$ has more pieces, so it is the larger fraction.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"more-pieces-means-larger"},{"text":"$6$ was added to $2$ and $9$ was added to $3$.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"added-instead-of-multiplied"},{"text":"Both $2$ and $3$ were multiplied by $4$, so each third became $4$ twelfths.","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$Both $2$ and $3$ were multiplied by $4$, so each third became $4$ twelfths.$q$, $q$Multiplying the numerator and the denominator by the same number, $\frac{2 \times 4}{3 \times 4} = \frac{8}{12}$, cuts every third into $4$ smaller pieces without changing the amount. Adding different amounts to the top and bottom is not a reason two fractions are equal, and more pieces does not mean a larger amount when the pieces are smaller.$q$, 'draft', null)
