   $q$A data set has a mean of 12 and a standard deviation of 4. If every value is multiplied by 3, what is the new standard deviation?$q$, $q$[{"text":"$4$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"thought-scaling-leaves-sd"},{"text":"$7$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"added-instead-of-multiplied"},{"text":"$36$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"scaled-sd-by-square-of-factor"},{"text":"$12$","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$$12$$q$, $q$Multiplying every value by 3 also multiplies every distance from the mean by 3, so the standard deviation becomes $4 \times 3 = 12$. The value $36$ multiplies by $3^2 = 9$, which is what happens to the variance, not the standard deviation.$q$, 'draft', null)
