  ('4b38247a-70be-57e8-8b2d-a226c49fc6c8', null, (select id from public.skills where slug = 'data-statistics'), null, 'hard',
   $q$Set P is 0, 10, 10, 10, 20 and Set Q is 0, 0, 10, 20, 20. Both sets have a mean of 10 and a range of 20. Which statement is true?$q$, $q$[{"text":"The standard deviations cannot be compared without the medians.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"thought-sd-needs-median"},{"text":"The sets have the same standard deviation because they have the same range.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"equated-range-with-sd"},{"text":"Set P has the greater standard deviation.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"reversed-spread-comparison"},{"text":"Set Q has the greater standard deviation.","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$Set Q has the greater standard deviation.$q$, $q$In Set P, three values sit right at the mean, so the squared deviations add to $100 + 0 + 0 + 0 + 100 = 200$. In Set Q, four values are $10$ away from the mean, so they add to $400$. More values far from the mean means a greater standard deviation for Set Q, even though the ranges match.$q$, 'draft', null)
