  ('9ec9b6f4-03ce-5722-9130-6441e7a9f5e7', null, (select id from public.skills where slug = 'data-statistics'), null, 'hard',
   $q$A dot plot shows how many books 11 students read over the summer: 2 students read 1 book, 3 read 2 books, 2 read 3 books, 1 read 4 books, 2 read 6 books, and 1 read 9 books. Find the quartiles by leaving the median out of both halves. What is the interquartile range?$q$, $q$[{"text":"$8$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"computed-range-instead"},{"text":"$4$","is_correct":true},{"text":"$6$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"reported-q3-as-iqr"},{"text":"$3$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"included-median-in-halves"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$$4$$q$, $q$List the data in order: 1, 1, 2, 2, 2, 3, 3, 4, 6, 6, 9. The median is the sixth value, $3$. The lower half 1, 1, 2, 2, 2 has $Q_1 = 2$, and the upper half 3, 4, 6, 6, 9 has $Q_3 = 6$. The IQR is $6 - 2 = 4$. The value $3$ comes from putting the median into both halves, which gives $Q_3 = 5$.$q$, 'draft', null)
