  ('b0ad7b5b-71ef-58ed-b4c3-1c2339be11e4', null, (select id from public.skills where slug = 'data-statistics'), null, 'medium',
   $q$Over many races, Runner A's 5-kilometer times have a mean of 24 minutes and a standard deviation of 0.5 minutes. Runner B's times have a mean of 23 minutes and a standard deviation of 2.5 minutes. Which runner's times are more consistent?$q$, $q$[{"text":"Runner B, because the mean time is faster.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"confused-faster-with-consistent"},{"text":"Both are equally consistent, because the means are close.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"ignored-sd-values"},{"text":"Runner A, because the standard deviation is smaller.","is_correct":true},{"text":"It cannot be told from the mean and standard deviation.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"thought-sd-not-about-consistency"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$Runner A, because the standard deviation is smaller.$q$, $q$Consistency is about spread. Runner A's standard deviation of $0.5$ minutes is much smaller than Runner B's $2.5$ minutes, so Runner A's times stay closer to that runner's mean. Runner B is faster on average, but a lower mean says nothing about how much the times vary.$q$, 'draft', null)
