  ('061910d0-8025-566e-bf17-e2a5035a406c', null, (select id from public.skills where slug = 'data-statistics'), null, 'hard',
   $q$A set of 9 values has a mean of 50 and a population standard deviation of 6. A tenth value, exactly 50, is added. What happens to the mean and the population standard deviation?$q$, $q$[{"text":"The mean stays the same and the standard deviation decreases.","is_correct":true},{"text":"The mean and the standard deviation both stay the same.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"thought-mean-value-leaves-sd-unchanged"},{"text":"The mean increases and the standard deviation stays the same.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"thought-adding-a-value-raises-mean"},{"text":"The mean stays the same and the standard deviation increases.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"thought-more-data-means-more-spread"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$The mean stays the same and the standard deviation decreases.$q$, $q$The new sum is $450 + 50 = 500$, so the mean is still $\frac{500}{10} = 50$. The new value has a deviation of $0$, so the sum of squared deviations stays the same but is now divided by 10 instead of 9. The variance drops from $36$ to $32.4$, so the standard deviation decreases.$q$, 'draft', null)
